GPS Module Overview

Use to determine the geographic velocity, position, and time of receiver by using information from multiple satellites[1].
This system uses a method called trilateration, it uses the time traveled to be able to calculate all three spatial dimensions, and the time dimension.
The actual system is made up of three components: space component, control component, and user component.  
Satellites are situated 20Km above the earth.
Receivers need the view of at least four satellites to perform the calculations.
However, this portion of the GPS is not important currently, what matters if the performance of the receiver, and whether or not it meets the criteria we need.

Pulled from the Spirent GNSS Manuel, it defines these five criteria as most important:

Cold Start TTFF: Time taken by a receiver to
acquire the signals and perform the position fix
(time, position and ephemeris parameters are all
unknown). This is an important metric to ensure that
a vehicle is operational quickly after a long parking
time or after towing.
• Hot Start TTFF: Same as cold start but the DUT
already knows position (within 100km of last fix),
time, ephemeris and almanac. Short hot start TTFF
allows a vehicle to become operational quickly after
shorter-term parking.
• Horizontal Position Accuracy (HPA)—: 50% of
measured horizontal position values located inside
a circle of radius equal to the CEP value. It should
be noted that RMS can be considered to hold
greater scientific insights into performance as it is
more stringent and can consider ground truth data,
but within this paper CEP50 is measured to allow
for comparability with the published specifications.
• Acquisition Sensitivity: Minimum received power
level at which a ‘First Fix’ can occur. A good
acquisition sensitivity supports the fast picking up of
GNSS signals after the vehicle exits a GNSS denied
environment such as a tunnel.
• Tracking Sensitivity: Minimum power level at which
a DUT can maintain lock. Good performance on
tracking sensitivity allows a vehicle to maintain
accurate positioning even in challenging
environments (e.g. obstructed signals).

However, these are more for the spatial components regarding gps, these are secondary to our objective of timer transfer.

According to NIST (National Institute of Standards and Technology), these criteria are more important:

•	Propagation Delay: It takes time for a signal to travel from the satellite and the receiver, so that must be accounted for. This is further exacerbated by atmospheric conditions, multipathing, and hardware delays.

From these two sources, I can converge on these criteria to be most important for our capstone:
•	Accuracy: Difference between the source clock (obviously)
•	Timing for synchronization: How long does the system take for the clock to achieve synchronization.
•	Drift: How much does the clock drift when it loses connection with the GPS
•	GPS Lock: How much time does the GPS provide useful data
•	Signal Acquisition Time: How long it takes for the module to acquire a signal after start or loss\


